					"heading": "The Real Astronomy Behind Apparent Retrograde Motion",
					"body": "Mercury never actually reverses its physical orbit around the Sun. Because Mercury is the innermost planet, it orbits the Sun every 88 Earth days—traveling at nearly 29 miles per second compared to Earth's 18.5 miles per second. Three or four times a year, as Mercury overtakes Earth on the inside track, it appears from our vantage point on Earth to slow down ('station'), drift backward from east to west against the background stars for 21 days, and then resume forward ('direct') motion."
